Allergic Conjunctivitis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Bilateral intense pruritus, Watery discharge, Conjunctival hyperemia

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-007
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of severe bilateral eye swelling and unremitting itch.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an explosive onset of severe bilateral periorbital edema and intense ocular distress, rendering the patient unable to open their eyes comfortably. Given the sudden, dramatic presentation of facial and periorbital swelling, an immediate on-site clinical assessment is required to definitively rule out sight-threatening conditions such as acute orbital cellulitis, severe contact dermatitis, or an evolving anaphylactic reaction. Delaying evaluation until the next standard operating period presents an unacceptable clinical risk of missing a rapidly progressive orbital complication or severe systemic hypersensitivity, thereby satisfying the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: My eyes swelled shut within an hour, and they are itching so badly I want to tear them out.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 4 hours ago with mild bilateral ocular pruritus after spending time outdoors. This rapidly progressed over the next 2 hours into an intense, unrelenting itch accompanied by profuse, watery, non-purulent ocular discharge. The patient reports significant swelling of the conjunctiva and edematous, puffy eyelids bilaterally. Denies any changes in visual acuity, photophobia, severe deep ocular pain, or purulent crusting. Acknowledges a remote history of seasonal atopy but states this episode is unprecedented in severity. Frequent sneezing and clear rhinorrhea began concurrently.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently visually restricted due to severe eyelid edema and reflex tearing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute distress due to transient visual impairment and severe discomfort.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 126/80 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 71 Kg
General: Alert. Sitting in a darkened room, vigorously rubbing bilateral closed eyes. Acute distress visible secondary to severe pruritus.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available.
Physical Exam: Bilateral periorbital edema is marked. Visual acuity is grossly intact bilaterally (assessed once eyelids manually retracted). Pupils are equal and reactive to light. Extraocular movements are full and painless. Severe bilateral conjunctival hyperemia is present. The conjunctiva is distinctly swollen (chemosis). Eversion of the upper eyelids reveals a pronounced papillary reaction on the tarsal conjunctiva. Discharge is strictly watery and clear; no purulence observed. Lung fields are clear to auscultation; no wheeze.
Environmental Observations: Residence is secure. Windows are open, potentially allowing ongoing aeroallergen exposure.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient flushed eyes with tap water with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Allergic Conjunctivitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Orbital Cellulitis - Ruled out definitively due to the bilateral presentation, intact and painless extraocular movements, normal pupillary reflexes, and absence of fever or deep, aching ocular pain.
Viral Conjunctivitis - Unlikely given the explosive onset, the intense predominance of pruritus over gritty foreign-body sensation, and the absence of a preauricular lymphadenopathy.
Bacterial Conjunctivitis - Ruled out due to the strictly watery, clear discharge rather than thick, opaque purulence, and the primary complaint of intense itch rather than pain or matting of the eyelashes.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The explosive onset of significant periorbital edema and severe ocular distress raised immediate red flags for an evolving sight-threatening or systemic allergic emergency. Urgent on-site assessment was mandated to perform a focused ophthalmologic and neurological examination to rule out orbital cellulitis or an atypical presentation of anaphylaxis. Physical examination confirmed severe but localized allergic conjunctivitis with prominent chemosis. Immediate intervention with potent topical antihistamine/mast-cell stabilizers was required to arrest the allergic cascade and relieve the incapacitating pruritus. This rapid clinical stabilization and definitive exclusion of emergencies could not be safely deferred to routine consulting hours, strictly fulfilling MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered Olopatadine 0.1% eye drops bilaterally stat. Prescribed Olopatadine 0.1% drops, 1 drop to each eye twice daily. Advised oral Cetirizine 10mg daily for concurrent rhinitis symptoms.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce the severe itch, apply a clean, ice-cold compress across closed eyes for 10 minutes every hour. The cold directly constricts the blood vessels in the eye, reducing swelling, while simultaneously numbing the nerve endings that transmit the urge to rub. Strictly avoid rubbing the eyes, as mechanical friction ruptures mast cells, flooding the eye with more histamine and worsening the condition immediately.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 3 days if symptoms do not significantly improve or if visual changes occur.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the Emergency Department immediately if experiencing any loss of vision, severe deep eye pain, inability to move the eye, or swelling of the lips and tongue.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on acute clinical presentation.
Clinical Course: Symptoms should sharply improve within hours of initiating topical antihistamine therapy. Complete resolution depends on removal from the offending aeroallergen.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate review if the discharge turns thick and yellow/green, or if you develop a severe, aching pain behind the eyeball.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: An allergen has triggered an explosive release of histamine in your eyes, causing the vessels to leak fluid and the nerves to itch violently. The prescribed drops block the histamine receptors directly on the eye surface, stopping the reaction at the source.
Actionable Steps: Keep windows closed during high pollen periods. Store the eye drops in the refrigerator; instilling chilled drops provides immediate, soothing relief to inflamed tissues.

